<aside name="forwardEulerAlgorith"><title>Forward Euler algorithm:</title>

<p>Let <m>x(t)</m> be the solution to a pure-time differential equation given by
<md>
  <mrow name="pure_time">\frac{ dx }{ dt }\amp = f(t)</mrow>
  <mrow>x(t_0)\amp = x_0, \notag</mrow>
</md>
Then, the function <m>x(t)</m> can be approximated for time steps of length <m>\Delta t</m> by a series of linear approximations given by:
  <md>
    <mrow> x(t_{i+1}) \approx x(t_i) + x'(t_i)\Delta t\;\;\;\text{for} \;\;\;i = 0,1,2,...</mrow>
  </md></p>
    
 
</aside>

  

<problem><title>Video review questions</title>
<setup>

</setup>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>The fundamental idea behind the Forward Euler algorithm for approximating a solution to a differential equation is
  <answer name="ans1a">
    <choice>to sketch a graph of the solution function based on where the graph of the differential equation is positive, negative, or zero.</choice>
    <choice>to use intuition to check possible solution functions and see if they match the match the differential equation when they are differentiated.</choice>
    <choice credit="1">to use the slope of the solution function as calculated from the differential equation as an approximation to the function itself over a series of small time steps.</choice>
  </answer></p>
  <feedback condition="$ans1a = $_choice1.text">This is a good way to gather information about the behavior of the solution, as illustrated in <ref to="doenet:7TzyuEb4GCMtxb3GbDoU4T">Graphical solution of pure-time differential equations</ref>. The Forward Euler algorithm, however, describes a different process for gathering information about the solution function. See <ref to="$v1">Key Moment 1</ref></feedback>
  <feedback condition="$ans1a = $_choice2.text">This option can be the perfect way to find a solution to a simple differential equation, as illustrated in <ref to="doenet:7TnsSPfdT3XFW3iGJ1EoWM">Introduction to pure-time differential equations</ref>. The Forward Euler algorithm, however, describes a different process for gathering information about the solution function. It can also be used for much more complex functions that do not lend themselves to a guess-and-check procedure for discovery. See <ref to="$v1">Key Moment 1</ref>
  </feedback>
<hint>See <ref to="$vid_section">Key Moment 1</ref></hint>

</exercise>

